WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.140
Google just demoed an ai that builds a whole interactive 3D world with a flying mouse at a Victorian

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:12.360
tea party in real time. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, episode 210. Invented Chatbot details,

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:18.440
a gift experiment you can run today, and quick bits are on the way. Google DeepMind shared a demo on

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:25.140
X of Genie 4, a system that turns a type sentence into a living 3D scene you can walk through. You type

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:29.560
type something ordinary, like a mouse at a Victorian high T, and the scene shows up with

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:30.380
moving characters.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.660
Weather can shift, sound can start, the place does not sit there like a frozen screenshot.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:41.100
Add a new instruction, such as the mouse grows wings and flies to the teacup, and the world

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.860
updates while you keep looking around.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:48.180
A porcupine walks by in the same demo, and you can swing the camera freely as if you were

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:48.960
inside a game.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.400
Picture a video game level that builds the next room the moment you open the door.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.980
No level designer is sitting there with you.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.840
That used to take teams of game developers and artists working for months.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:01.960
Here are the building happens as you play.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:06.200
Behind the curtain, another Google system called Gemini acts as the brain.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:11.540
Gemini is Google's general purpose model, the one that reads your words and decides what

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:12.340
should happen next.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:15.240
Think of Gemini as a theater director backstage.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.100
You shout a change from the seats, and the director restages the actors, the lights,

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:21.140
and even the chatter.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:26.700
It plans complex events, watches the scene unfold, and adjusts when you change the story

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.240
so the plot does not fall apart.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:33.700
It also writes natural dialogue and background audio on the spot, so the room has voices

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:35.560
and atmosphere, not just pictures.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:41.920
Genie 4 adds ambient sound, meaning the hum of a place, plus human conversation that can

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:42.840
change as you talk.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:48.400
Earlier world models often had no sound, or they got wobbly if you stayed in the scene for

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:49.040
a long stretch.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:54.300
This mix of plain language control, steady physics, and built-in audio is the jump

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.960
testers keep pointing at. Steady physics is like the unspoken rules in Minecraft.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:04.640
Block still fall, path still connect, the world does not melt every time you add a

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:09.680
sentence. Early testers are trying it for filmmaking, game design, education, and

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:14.240
robotic simulation, which is practiced for robots in a fake space before the real

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:21.680
world. One uses VFX pre-visualization. VFX means visual effects, the movie magic of creatures

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:27.140
and impossible shots, and pre-visualization is a rough version of a shot before anyone films

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:32.980
it. Instead of a paper storyboard, you could walk around the shot. Another use is bringing a historical

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:39.360
moment to life from verified archives, like a field trip into a documented day, not a random daydream.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:39.460
Game.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:45.020
Tester said the biggest leaps were more photorealism, which means it looks closer to a real camera,

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:49.060
plus text that can change inside the scene, and that live audio.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:53.660
This is AI moving off a chat window and into places that feel occupied.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:59.300
If you love games, stories, or messing around with ideas, that is a different kind of toy.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.200
Students could one day walk through a history lesson.

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:07.480
Friends could co-create a short animated story in minutes instead of waiting on a whole studio.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:12.680
video. The model has strict safety guardrails, meaning built-in limits on what it will create.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:18.660
All generated audio and video is watermarked with Synth ID. Synth ID is a hidden stamp,

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:23.540
like a school poster with an invisible mark that still proves which printer made it. So

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:29.300
viewers can tell the clip came from AI. Genie 4 is still in early testing. You cannot try

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:34.620
the full version yet. It is set to roll out first to Google AI Ultra Subscribers, that

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:39.380
That is Google's paid ultra plan, and to some partners through Project Genie and the Gemini

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:39.860
API.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:41.700
An API is a doorway.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.940
Other apps send a request through that door, and Gemini sends a result back without

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:48.880
you living inside Google's own screen.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:54.400
Short clips are already online, and this kind of progress often shows up later in free or

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.220
cheaper tools.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:58.880
Until the full thing is public, those clips are the closest look.

00:03:59.988 --> 00:04:04.488
Ancient Greek writing is a surprisingly good teacher for why a chat bot sometimes invents

00:04:04.488 --> 00:04:09.348
a detail when the facts run out. A reddit thread laid this out by borrowing a habit from

00:04:09.348 --> 00:04:14.348
classical scholars, the people who spend careers on tiny surviving scraps of old books.

00:04:14.948 --> 00:04:19.648
Picture a group chat where you forgot what someone said last Tuesday. To keep the conversation

00:04:19.648 --> 00:04:24.728
moving, your brain supplies the most likely missing bit. You still know somewhere that you

00:04:24.728 --> 00:04:29.748
guessed. A language model, which is a system trained to predict the next word, does the

00:04:29.748 --> 00:04:34.688
Philan moved to. The twist is it has no sticky note for which words it actually read and

00:04:34.688 --> 00:04:39.508
which words it only invented so the sentence would sound finished. Of everything the thinker

00:04:39.508 --> 00:04:45.308
Thali supposedly wrote, not one authenticated word survives. Authenticated means we can trust

00:04:45.308 --> 00:04:50.108
it is really his. An axiomander wrote the first prose treatise in the Greek tradition,

00:04:50.688 --> 00:04:55.628
a treatise being a serious written argument, not a poem. Today that work exists as roughly

00:04:55.628 --> 00:05:01.068
one sentence, saved because Aristotle quoted it, and simplistic as copied that quotation

00:05:01.068 --> 00:05:02.628
almost a thousand years later.

00:05:03.348 --> 00:05:08.868
Heraclitus reaches us as scattered aphorisms, short punchy sayings, and most of those sit

00:05:08.868 --> 00:05:12.968
inside sermons by church fathers who quoted him in order to argue against him.

00:05:13.548 --> 00:05:15.448
Scholars made a simple classroom rule.

00:05:15.988 --> 00:05:20.068
When they guess a missing word, they put the guess in brackets, or in a different font,

00:05:20.488 --> 00:05:22.528
so nobody confuses evidence with a hunch.

00:05:22.528 --> 00:05:26.348
It is like a group project where each person uses a different highlighter.

00:05:26.848 --> 00:05:30.108
You can see who wrote the solid part and who penciled in a maybe.

00:05:30.708 --> 00:05:34.048
The standard edition of those early thinkers is called Deals Crans.

00:05:34.548 --> 00:05:36.188
It physically separates fragments.

00:05:36.748 --> 00:05:41.968
The bits the philosopher actually wrote from Testimonia, which are later writers talking about him.

00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:44.928
Aristotle also split two kinds of support.

00:05:45.768 --> 00:05:48.268
Necessary evidence is the kind that forces a conclusion.

00:05:48.268 --> 00:05:52.628
A probable sign only leans toward a claim and leaves the door open.

00:05:53.168 --> 00:05:55.828
Modern language models do not grow up with that habit.

00:05:56.288 --> 00:06:00.848
Their training rewards smooth, finish-sounding answers, like a student who never leaves

00:06:00.848 --> 00:06:01.948
a blank on a test.

00:06:02.468 --> 00:06:06.848
When your prompt runs out of clear facts, the model keeps predicting the next likely word.

00:06:07.348 --> 00:06:09.988
It does not mark the moment it started making things up.

00:06:10.408 --> 00:06:15.428
The result is fluent text that mixes real information with confident sounding guesses, and

00:06:15.428 --> 00:06:16.888
the page never changes color.

00:06:16.888 --> 00:06:22.188
That is why the same model can quote a real historical sentence correctly, then continue

00:06:22.188 --> 00:06:25.088
with invented details, all in the same calm voice.

00:06:25.648 --> 00:06:27.948
Auto-complete on your phone wants to finish a word.

00:06:28.448 --> 00:06:32.868
These systems want to finish a whole answer, even when the honest ending would be, I do

00:06:32.868 --> 00:06:33.308
not know.

00:06:33.968 --> 00:06:38.668
Researchers are trying to add brackets inside the model itself, such as confidence scores,

00:06:38.988 --> 00:06:42.188
which are honesty ratings, or a requirement to cite sources.

00:06:42.768 --> 00:06:45.708
The older scholarly tradition says the failure is structural.

00:06:45.708 --> 00:06:50.888
The system has no inner sense of evidential weight, meaning how heavy the proof is.

00:06:51.308 --> 00:06:53.688
Partial fixes get bolted on from the outside.

00:06:54.348 --> 00:06:58.528
One is citation grounded generation, where the writing has to point at a source.

00:06:59.128 --> 00:07:03.728
Another is retrieval augmented generation, which means the model looks up documents first,

00:07:04.268 --> 00:07:06.328
then writes and can label those documents.

00:07:06.968 --> 00:07:10.768
Those are backpacks strapped onto the model, not a new bone in its skeleton.

00:07:11.468 --> 00:07:16.308
Preference Raiders, the people who score answers, reward fluent completeness, and nobody

00:07:16.308 --> 00:07:17.348
upvotes a bracket.

00:07:17.908 --> 00:07:22.228
Once you read a chat bot like a draft with invisible pencil marks, the confident tone

00:07:22.228 --> 00:07:23.448
gets easier to question.

00:07:23.968 --> 00:07:25.468
That is a skill, not a vibe.

00:07:25.988 --> 00:07:27.808
You can feel this yourself in about a minute.

00:07:28.328 --> 00:07:33.188
Go to the chat GPT website, start a new chat, and type a question about what Thales wrote.

00:07:33.728 --> 00:07:38.248
Then send a second message that says, put brackets around every sentence that is a guess

00:07:38.248 --> 00:07:39.608
rather than a sourced quote.

00:07:39.608 --> 00:07:44.408
Watch whether the voice stays equally sure and whether any brackets appear at all.

00:07:45.176 --> 00:07:50.516
Chat GPT and Claude can also help when you are stuck on a gift and the store pages all blur together.

00:07:51.056 --> 00:07:54.956
They are chatbots, meaning you type in ordinary language and they type back.

00:07:55.376 --> 00:08:00.916
Money control walk through this with gift ideas for a celebration called Car what Chouth in 2026

00:08:00.916 --> 00:08:06.356
and the same method works for a birthday, a festival or any day you want to look like you paid attention.

00:08:06.356 --> 00:08:08.996
You describe the person and what they like.

00:08:09.356 --> 00:08:14.296
The chatbot suggests options, and it can add why each one might fit, which is the part

00:08:14.296 --> 00:08:16.216
of random search bar usually skips.

00:08:16.856 --> 00:08:20.016
Anyone who has stared at a blank search box can try this right now.

00:08:20.596 --> 00:08:25.156
Go to the chat GPT website or Claude's website, start a new chat and type this.

00:08:25.776 --> 00:08:28.576
My wife loves reading fantasy novels and drinking tea.

00:08:29.076 --> 00:08:33.516
Suggest three thoughtful gifts under $50 and explain why each one might work.

00:08:33.516 --> 00:08:38.656
That is like texting a friend who has wandered every mall and still asks follow-up questions.

00:08:39.156 --> 00:08:43.996
If the first list feels generic, add favorite colors, hobbies, or a recent book they loved,

00:08:44.356 --> 00:08:49.716
and ask again, keep the back and forth going until one idea feels like them, not like a

00:08:49.716 --> 00:08:50.456
random object.

00:08:51.036 --> 00:08:55.276
The free tier most people already have is enough, no code, no special setup.

00:08:56.417 --> 00:09:00.357
Hundreds of people on Reddit were asked what a fair social contract would look like if

00:09:00.357 --> 00:09:02.437
AI and robots handled most jobs.

00:09:02.437 --> 00:09:07.137
A social contract here means the basic deal a society agrees to live by.

00:09:07.537 --> 00:09:09.537
The top answer was not a single rule.

00:09:10.057 --> 00:09:11.717
It was the question, who decides?

00:09:12.517 --> 00:09:16.717
People wanted regular voices, not only big companies, to have a say in how the tools

00:09:16.717 --> 00:09:17.317
get used.

00:09:17.897 --> 00:09:21.157
Other common asks were enforcement so rules actually bite.

00:09:21.757 --> 00:09:26.477
A basic income floor during the shift, meaning some guaranteed money while work changes.

00:09:26.477 --> 00:09:31.577
images, the right to live without AI, a ban on autonomous killing machines, which are

00:09:31.577 --> 00:09:35.937
weapons that can choose a target without a person, and a voice for people outside rich

00:09:35.937 --> 00:09:36.397
countries.

00:09:36.937 --> 00:09:41.597
A farmer in his 60s from Korea posted the original question and noted that none of those

00:09:41.597 --> 00:09:43.477
six ideas had been in his own book.

00:09:44.424 --> 00:09:50.224
Texas is suing open AI, Cyber News reports, saying the company did not properly warn users,

00:09:50.484 --> 00:09:54.724
including kids, that chat GPT can give inaccurate or harmful answers.

00:09:54.724 --> 00:10:00.344
Attorney General Ken Paxton alleges open AI put growth and market value ahead of user

00:10:00.344 --> 00:10:06.084
safety, and he wants compliance with Texas Consumer Protection laws and child online safety

00:10:06.084 --> 00:10:06.424
laws.

00:10:06.944 --> 00:10:12.384
The lawsuit points to reports of harms including mass shootings, public humiliation, suicide

00:10:12.384 --> 00:10:16.244
and addiction, and it claims those risks were not disclosed to users.

00:10:16.824 --> 00:10:24.004
The filing says open AI was valued at $17 billion in 2022 and has since risen to over $1.4

00:10:24.004 --> 00:10:29.644
trillion dollars. That's it for today. Remember, every AI expert started exactly where you are

00:10:29.644 --> 00:10:34.964
right now. If something we talked about today made you curious, go try it. That's literally how

